Xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:

>>...let's leave stereotypes behind and focus on gender differences
>>that might make >>the game more fun to play (if that's possible ...).

>Well, dexterity versus main strength is a pretty obvious one.  How
>about the women get +1 to hit with the smaller weapons, where agility
>counts, and the men get +1 to damage with the larger ones where moving
>mass in a hurry counts?

This depends a lot on whether you want realism or not.  I don't be-
lieve women in real life have the agility edge over men, and they
have a decided size disadvantage.

>How about sneaking by boulders? Maybe a gal could get past in some of
>her armor/with some of her pack, but couldn't budge the silly thing,
>while a guy couldn't get past, but at the price of 5/1 increased food
>consumption, could move it down the passage?

This would be "realistic" in my opinion.  I keep harping on the caloric
intake thing, but in real life women need to eat decidedly less food
than men, and, and are slightly better at enduring long-term depriva-
tion.

>A woman should be able to recognize some jewels and potions on sight;
>a man should be able to kill a monster without making it inedible (gone)
>more often.

It is true that women tend to wear more jewels than men, but I wonder
whether this is getting too subtle.  Why should they be able to recog-
nize them?

>Women should be more knowledgeable about hats, men about boots.

Only men from Texas.  No, really, my wife hasn't worn a hat since I've
known her, except a ski cap to keep her head warm in the snow (which
I've worn as well).  None of my sisters wore hats.  Nor my mom.  I
wonder...  Aren't we getting a bit too subtle, and deviating from
reality a bit too much?

>Women should be more successful at reading, men at fighting.

Again, I isn't this getting too subtle?  I dunno, but I don't see any
evidence that women are any better at reading than men, and I'd hate
to see such an odd stereotype sent down into the dungeon.

>Women should occasionally have an "intuition" about a cursed or blessed
>object, or a secret door.  Men should be better at training pets.

The winner of the Ididerwhatever race has been a woman for the last sev-
eral years.  I dunno.  I think we need to hone this thing down a bit,
rather than perpetuate myths like womens' intuition.  Maybe I'm coming
off as a bit cranky.  You see, I married this gal who is great at num-
bers.  She also has a terriffic memory, and likes trying new things.
I, on the other hand, have no such aptitude for pure math.  But I can
read three or four languages, and I have a much better memory and feel-
ing for people and faces than my wife.

When I sat down to think of how to represent gender (not sexual) dif-
ferences in NetHack, I decided that the main distinction was one of
strength and size.  Although I am not an athlete, and my wife is very
tall for a woman (5'10") - in fact I am overweight and out of shape -
I am *still* much, much stronger than her, and my agility is also
better.

The problem here is that in the dungeon, strength and agility are
at a premium.  And if we are going to make women less strong and agile,
then we are going to have a helluva time making up for the difference
in the simple and clear language of NetHack character traits.

Of the suggestions I've seen, I personally only like the ideas:  1)
Make women resistent to nymphs, 2) make them better able to squeeze
through small spaces, 3) make them need less food (or take much longer
to starve), and 4) make them stealthier.

Would this make up for the strength deficit?  Would it also make play-
ing the character more enjoyable?  Dunno.  I tend not to like solu-
tions to the strength-deficit problem which rely on traits that are
not clear, demonstrable physical differences.

-Richard


